      "math_academy_id": "q-94825",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{4}^{2})$ with $P(X<3)=0.6368,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3)=0.6368\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 3)=0.6368\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.6368 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6368 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3-\\mu }{4}) & =0.6368 \\\\ \\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6368)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.35)=0.6368\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6368)=0.35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3-\\mu }{4} & =0.35 \\\\ 3-\\mu  & =1.4 \\\\ \\mu  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
